{
  "doc": [
    "twoody/vault/device-access/v1 (docs/mesh-design.md §1 rule 5, lot P0). Reference: src/vault/contracts.ts (signDeviceAccess, deviceAccessValid).",
    "A person-device (the phone) states with its own vault signing key that a tunnel key is its own, so that a trusted computer links it without the account token deciding anything.",
    "Signature scheme: the v1 vault approval's — Ed25519 (RFC 8032) by the private half of the vault device's sign_public, over the canonical header: UTF-8 of the label 'twoody/vault/device-access/v1', then for each field the UTF-8 of String(field) prefixed by its byte length on 2 bytes big-endian. Fields: [v, user_id, vault_device_id, tunnel_public_key] — v = '1' like every other vault statement (the design's short list [user, vault_device_id, tunnel_public_key] plus the version).",
    "Field encodings: user_id decimal ('42'); vault_device_id the phone's vault_devices uuid; tunnel_public_key the phone's X25519 tunnel key, 64 lowercase hex (the key it pairs with, POST /api/v1/session tunnel_public_key). Signature: 128 lowercase hex.",
    "Wire form: {vault_device_id, tunnel_public_key, signature}; the verifier takes the signer's sign_public from the verified roster entry of vault_device_id. Rules for verifiers: vault_device_id must be a trusted member of the verified roster that is a person-device (no binding, or a binding with can_approve=true); a statement for a computer (binding can_approve=false) is refused even when its signature checks; the tunnel key presented on the link must equal tunnel_public_key.",
    "Keys: those of computer-binding-v1.json (same devices, same derivation); iphone_tunnel is the phone's X25519 tunnel key."
  ],
